## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a branchless, constant-time population-count across 128 bits using only shifts and masks, a common low-level optimization in crypto/signature code.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register)
- 64-bit popcount, extended to 128-bit
- Branchless arithmetic for performance

## Code Example

```c
unsigned int popcount64(uint64_t w) {
  w = w - ((w >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  w = (w & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((w >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  w = (w + (w >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (unsigned int)((w * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56);
}
unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
  unsigned int hi = popcount64(x.hi);
  unsigned int lo = popcount64(x.lo);
  return hi + lo;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for variable-width counts (e.g., 256-bit)?
- What are the trade-offs compared to using hardware popcnt when available?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests cross-boundary bit manipulation and 128-bit arithmetic, which are fundamental skills in cryptography and high-performance kernel development. It also evaluates the ability to implement rotation operations without branching logic.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit rotation across two 64-bit limbs
- Using a single 128-bit intermediate simplifies boundary handling
- Edge-case handling via rotation count masking (eliminates branches)

## Code Example

```cpp
void rotl128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned int k, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  __uint128_t v = ((__uint128_t)hi << 64) | lo;
  k &= 127;
  __uint128_t r = (v << k) | (v >> (128 - k));
  *out_hi = (uint64_t)(r >> 64);
  *out_lo = (uint64_t)r;
}
```

## Edge Cases

- `k = 0`: Returns original value unchanged
- `k = 64`: Swaps hi and lo parts completely

## Why This Is Asked
Tests extending known 64-bit tricks to 128-bit values with branchless code.

## Key Concepts
- Branchless bit counting
- 64-bit popcount kernel
- Combining results across two words

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x){
  x -= (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

uint8_t popcount128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo){
  return (uint8_t)(popcount64(hi) + popcount64(lo));
}
```

